s","route":{"from_name":"Atlanta","to_name":"Baton Rouge Metropolitan Airport"},"location":{"name":"Huntington","country":"United States"},"phase":"takeoff","occupants":null,"fatalities":75,"category":"CFIT","summary":"Southern Airways Flight 932, a McDonnell Douglas DC-9-30 operating a charter flight for the Marshall University football team from North Carolina to Huntington, West Virginia, crashed on November 14, 1970. The aircraft was carrying the football team returning from a game, plus coaches, staff, and boosters. During final approach to Tri-State Airport near Huntington, the controller advised of rain, fog, smoke, and a ragged ceiling. The aircraft was cleared to descend but failed to arrest its descent at the prescribed altitude. The aircraft descended approximately 300 feet below the minimum prescribed altitude, apparently without crew visual sighting of the airport. A nearby refinery at lower elevation and hills between the refinery and airport likely created visual illusion of altitude. The aircraft struck trees on a hillside 5,543 feet short of the runway threshold and crashed, killing all 75 aboard.","probable_cause":"Descent below minimum descent altitude during nonprecision approach under adverse weather conditions without required visual contact with runway environment. Contributing factors included visual illusion from difference in terrain elevation between refinery and airport, possible altimetry system error introducing water contamination, and high workload during approach phase.","factors":[{"id":"low_visibility","role":"contributing","evidence":"Rain, fog, and smoke at airport and approach area"},{"id":"descent_below_minimums","role":"initiating","evidence":"Aircraft continued descent 300 feet below minimum descent altitude"},{"id":"visual_illusion","role":"contributing","evidence":"Visual illusion from refinery lights at different elevation than airport"},{"id":"loss_of_situational_awareness","role":"contributing","evidence":"Crew unaware o